AWS Concepts Simplified: Load Balancer vs Target Group vs Auto Scaling Group

When working with AWS, these three terms often come up together—but each plays a unique role. Here’s a quick breakdown:

🔹 Load Balancer (LB)
 Distributes incoming traffic across multiple resources to ensure high availability and fault tolerance. Think of it as: “Who should handle this request?”

🔹 Target Group (TG)
 A collection of resources (EC2, IPs, containers, or Lambda) where the Load Balancer sends requests. Includes health checks to ensure only healthy targets receive traffic. Think of it as: “Where should the LB send the traffic?”

🔹 Auto Scaling Group (ASG)
 Manages a fleet of EC2 instances—scales up during high demand and scales down when demand drops, ensuring performance + cost optimization. Think of it as: “How many servers should be running?”

🔗 How they work together
 1️⃣ User request → Load Balancer
 2️⃣ Routed to → Target Group
 3️⃣ Instances inside TG managed by → Auto Scaling Group

✅ Example:
ALB receives traffic
Routes /api/* → Target Group A (backed by an ASG)
Routes /images/* → Target Group B (backed by another ASG)

These three services, when combined, enable elastic, scalable, and reliable architectures on AWS.
